WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.880
AI agents may retrieve documents, query databases, summarize records, retain preferences, or store task history.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:15.460
That capability can improve continuity, but it also creates confidentiality, integrity, privacy, and retention concerns.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:23.200
Security teams should classify the data involved and minimize what the agent receives rather than placing entire data stores into model context.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.500
Access checks must follow the data.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:34.200
A retrieval system should verify that the requesting user and agent are allowed to search a source, and it should apply authorization again before returning results.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.420
Filtering only after sensitive data reaches the model is too late.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.320
Responses and tool outputs should also be checked before information is shown, stored, or sent elsewhere.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:50.780
Memory may contain user preferences, summaries, prior decisions, tool results, or learned context.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.960
It can be sensitive, stale, incorrect, or influenced by untrusted input.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:05.780
Good memory controls include provenance, retention limits, validation, access restrictions, review and deletion paths, and separation between users or tenants.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.640
High-impact decisions should not rely on opaque memory without fresh verification.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:16.380
Secrets should be supplied through controlled credential mechanisms, not embedded in prompts or long-term memory.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.800
Logging should avoid unnecessary sensitive content while preserving enough context for investigation.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:31.780
Encryption, data-loss controls, redaction, and clear retention policies support safer handling, but the strongest first step is limiting which data the agent can access at all.
